  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 105 PM MDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nWidespread cloud cover has resulted in a wet, raw day across \neastern Idaho, with daytime temperatures struggling in the 40s \nand low 50s. Deep moisture continues to surge into the region \nfrom the south, fueled by the remnants of Tropical Cyclone \nRachel. This tropical moisture is interacting with cooler air \nfiltering down from the north as deep upper-level troughing \nbecomes firmly established over the Pacific Northwest.\n\nToday serves as the main event for precipitation. An inch or \nmore of rainfall is expected for most areas along and east of \nthe I-15 corridor. Localized spots could certainly see 2 to 3 \ninches, especially across the higher terrain of the eastern \nhighlands and around Island Park. Accordingly, a Flood Watch \nremains in place through tomorrow afternoon. High-resolution \nconvective-allowing models (CAMs) indicate a potential break in \nprecipitation later this evening before a secondary surge of \nmoisture advances into the area out of Nevada and Utah overnight\nand into Monday morning. Temperature forecast tonight is a bit \ntricky with cloud cover and precip in place. Nevertheless, a \nFreeze Warning has been issued for the Magic Valley and across \nthe Lava Beds and into the Arco Desert as these zones likely \nhave the best potential to see temps drop below freezing, \nbriefly. A Frost Advisory was issued for the Snake Plain with \nwidespread temps of 32-36 expected overnight. Not out of the \nquestion that some rural spots within these zones drop to near, \nor below freezing, but confidence is very low with all the \nclouds and precip forecast to be around. Either way, many nights\nahead with Frost/Freeze headlines expected followed by a \nsuspension of the growing season for the year later this week. \n\nModels continue to develop a closed low that will move slowly \nacross the Great Basin and into the Intermountain West between \nnow and mid-week. This synoptic feature will keep dense cloud \ncover and precipitation chances in place through at least \nTuesday. Following Monday morning's moisture surge, the \nprecipitation focus will shift toward the far southeastern \ncorner of the forecast area late Monday into Tuesday as the \nupper low tracks from Nevada into Utah. Conditions will remain \nseasonally chilly, with high temperatures predominantly in the \n50s.\n\nConditions will begin to steadily improve Wednesday and Thursday\nas the upper low slowly moves east of the region. While the\nECMWF (Euro) continues to hint at retrograding the low back into\nUtah, the vast majority of ensemble clusters suggest this is an\noutlier solution. Consequently, confidence remains moderately\nhigh that ridging will settle over the area later in the\nworkweek. This pattern shift will allow for clearing skies and\nmoderating temperatures, bringing highs back closer to seasonal\nnorms by Thursday or Friday. Following this week's raw weather,\nnext weekend is looking to be much more pleasant.",
